1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,864
This glass lens is about nine microns thick.

2
00:00:03,904 --> 00:00:06,743
This patterned plate is just one micron thick.

3
00:00:06,813 --> 00:00:08,192
Shine green light through both,

4
00:00:08,232 --> 00:00:11,256
and they focus it to exactly the same spot.

5
00:00:11,326 --> 00:00:14,749
But in white light, the glass lens still makes one sharp focus,

6
00:00:14,789 --> 00:00:18,052
while the plate smears the colours along its axis.

7
00:00:18,122 --> 00:00:22,483
How can one micron of glass do a thick lens's job? And what's the catch?

8
00:00:23,303 --> 00:00:25,780
Inside glass, light slows down to c/n,

9
00:00:25,820 --> 00:00:29,963
where n, the refractive index, is about 1.5.

10
00:00:30,033 --> 00:00:33,128
Follow two paths, one through a block of glass of height h,

11
00:00:33,168 --> 00:00:37,549
one through air. In the glass, the crests bunch up and fall behind.

12
00:00:37,619 --> 00:00:41,761
The glass path lags by (n − 1)h:

13
00:00:41,801 --> 00:00:48,545
a phase delay φ = k₀(n − 1)h, where k₀ = 2π/λ.

14
00:00:48,615 --> 00:00:52,732
If the glass is thin, each point is simply delayed on its own:

15
00:00:52,772 --> 00:00:57,381
the outgoing field is the incoming one, times e^(iφ(x)).

16
00:00:57,451 --> 00:00:58,922
That's the thin element approximation,

17
00:00:58,962 --> 00:01:02,287
TEA. Make the glass thickest in the middle,

18
00:01:02,327 --> 00:01:06,349
and a flat wave leaves curved, converging to a focus.

19
00:01:06,419 --> 00:01:09,295
It's the simplest, fastest rung on a ladder of models.

20
00:01:09,335 --> 00:01:13,062
Remember its one assumption: every point on its own.

21
00:01:13,882 --> 00:01:18,858
Now the key trick. Phase is an angle, so a delay of one full turn,

22
00:01:18,898 --> 00:01:22,634
2π, changes nothing: every crest lands where the next one was.

23
00:01:22,704 --> 00:01:26,507
In glass, one full turn of delay takes a height of λ/(n − 1).

24
00:01:26,547 --> 00:01:30,864
For green light, that's about 1.1 microns.

25
00:01:30,934 --> 00:01:34,116
So slice the lens into slabs of exactly that height.

26
00:01:34,186 --> 00:01:36,305
Each full slab only adds whole turns.

27
00:01:36,345 --> 00:01:38,963
It changes nothing, so throw it away.

28
00:01:39,033 --> 00:01:42,510
Keep just the curved remainders, and let them drop onto the base.

29
00:01:43,480 --> 00:01:45,420
What's left is a kinoform:

30
00:01:45,460 --> 00:01:48,855
rings called Fresnel zones, about one micron thick.

31
00:01:48,925 --> 00:01:52,841
The zones narrow toward the edge, where light must bend most steeply.

32
00:01:52,881 --> 00:01:55,590
There, they're only a few wavelengths wide.

33
00:01:55,660 --> 00:01:58,027
At the design wavelength, the kinoform

34
00:01:58,067 --> 00:02:01,706
multiplies the light by exactly the same e^(iφ).

35
00:02:01,746 --> 00:02:05,241
So it focuses to exactly the same spot.

36
00:02:06,061 --> 00:02:09,609
Real fabrication often makes just N discrete heights,

37
00:02:09,649 --> 00:02:11,687
so each zone becomes a staircase.

38
00:02:11,757 --> 00:02:14,737
Each zone works like a tooth of a grating.

39
00:02:14,777 --> 00:02:17,786
A perfect ramp sends all the light into one diffraction order:

40
00:02:17,826 --> 00:02:19,727
the one aimed at the focus.

41
00:02:19,797 --> 00:02:23,789
Steps leak light into other orders, and that light misses the focus.

42
00:02:23,859 --> 00:02:27,414
How much? At each point, draw an arrow for the gap between its

43
00:02:27,454 --> 00:02:31,120
phase and the perfect ramp, then add the arrows head to tail.

44
00:02:31,190 --> 00:02:32,780
For the ramp, they all line up.

45
00:02:32,820 --> 00:02:36,084
But each step holds its phase while the ramp keeps turning,

46
00:02:36,124 --> 00:02:39,662
so the chain curls, and its end-to-end arrow is shorter.

47
00:02:40,332 --> 00:02:44,761
Its squared length is the share of light in the focus: η = sinc²(1/N).

48
00:02:44,801 --> 00:02:53,404
That's 41% for two levels, 81% for four, 95% for eight, 99% for sixteen.

49
00:02:54,224 --> 00:02:57,147
Now colour. A kinoform works by diffraction:

50
00:02:57,187 --> 00:03:01,450
its zones are fixed, and longer wavelengths bend more at each one.

51
00:03:01,520 --> 00:03:05,173
So its focal length depends on colour,

52
00:03:05,213 --> 00:03:10,534
f = f₀λ₀/λ: red focuses closest, and blue farthest.

53
00:03:10,604 --> 00:03:13,408
A glass lens does the opposite, and far more gently:

54
00:03:13,448 --> 00:03:17,981
glass bends blue slightly more, so blue focuses a little closer.

55
00:03:18,051 --> 00:03:20,671
Opticians score this with the Abbe number:

56
00:03:20,711 --> 00:03:26,878
about +60 for common glass, but −3.45 for every diffractive lens.

57
00:03:26,948 --> 00:03:28,160
Since the signs are opposite,

58
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:32,703
a weak diffractive surface can cancel a glass lens's colour error.

59
00:03:32,743 --> 00:03:35,826
Or software can correct the colours afterwards.

60
00:03:36,646 --> 00:03:39,601
Now the deepest catch. The thin element approximation

61
00:03:39,641 --> 00:03:43,989
assumes every column of glass acts on its own. Is that true?

62
00:03:44,059 --> 00:03:48,555
Zoom in on zones four wavelengths wide, with walls two wavelengths tall.

63
00:03:49,225 --> 00:03:52,755
Solve Maxwell's equations directly, and the clean picture breaks.

64
00:03:52,795 --> 00:03:56,250
Light crowds along the walls, shoots out in jets,

65
00:03:56,290 --> 00:03:58,248
and leaves shadows beside them.

66
00:03:58,318 --> 00:04:03,016
So less light reaches the focus: TEA promises all of it,

67
00:04:03,056 --> 00:04:06,096
but the full solution delivers about 70%.

68
00:04:06,166 --> 00:04:08,567
The narrower the zones, the bigger the gap.

69
00:04:08,607 --> 00:04:11,424
Only zones many wavelengths wide are safe.

70
00:04:11,494 --> 00:04:14,318
Two ways out. Metasurfaces build the phase

71
00:04:14,358 --> 00:04:16,421
from pillars narrower than a wavelength,

72
00:04:16,461 --> 00:04:19,795
each one's delay computed from the full equations.

73
00:04:19,865 --> 00:04:22,182
Or drop the shortcut, and simulate the whole

74
00:04:22,222 --> 00:04:25,757
element with a full-wave solver. That's Episode 9.

75
00:04:26,577 --> 00:04:29,776
On a computer, a flat lens takes two steps:

76
00:04:29,816 --> 00:04:33,154
multiply the field by e^(ik₀(n − 1)h),

77
00:04:33,194 --> 00:04:38,185
then propagate it with the angular spectrum method from Episode 1.

78
00:04:38,255 --> 00:04:40,788
Three pitfalls: sample the finest zone

79
00:04:40,828 --> 00:04:42,831
with several points, wrap the heights,

80
00:04:42,871 --> 00:04:46,626
and simulate the profile you can really make, not the drawing.

81
00:04:46,696 --> 00:04:48,862
Blur this profile by a quarter of a micron,

82
00:04:48,902 --> 00:04:51,668
and a quarter of the focused light is gone.

83
00:04:52,488 --> 00:04:55,957
A thin element only delays light. Phase wraps at 2π,

84
00:04:55,997 --> 00:05:00,406
so a lens can be a micron thick, until “thin” stops being true.

85
00:05:00,476 --> 00:05:02,123
The price: steps scatter light,

86
00:05:02,163 --> 00:05:05,663
colours split apart, and fine zones stop being thin.

87
00:05:05,733 --> 00:05:08,867
And a lens's outer zones look a lot like a grating.

88
00:05:08,907 --> 00:05:12,451
So next time: what does periodic structure do to light?

